// TEST: Scalar Complex Intrinsic Units
// ============================================================================
// The SCALAR-position twin of units/065. There the cancelling complex product
// feeds a nested elementwise map (`exp <@> (i * w * ts)`), which routes through
// the kernel-apply path and was fixed by the deferral. Here the same product
// sits directly in a scalar call, `exp(i * w * tz)` -- and that path classified
// its operand on a BARE `IRTScalar` pattern.
//
// A unit-carrying complex is `IRTUnitAnnotated (IRTScalar ETComplex128, u)`, so
// it never matched the complex arm. It fell through to the general arm, which
// hardcodes a Float64 RESULT -- silently making the whole expression real. The
// damage surfaced at the next accessor, as `BL3007: real expects a complex
// operand, got Float64`, pointing at `real` rather than at the `exp` that
// dropped the complexity. `real`/`imag`/`arg` had the identical hole in the
// other direction: handed a genuine `Complex128<volts>` they rejected their own
// operand as not complex.
//
// Both now classify on the STRIPPED base and route the signature through the
// same `unitRulesForUnaryOp` table as everything else. The one shape that hid
// this: an expression whose complexity comes from a LITERAL `complex(...)`
// works either way, so lswosa.blade's `complex(...) * exp(i * w * t_zero)` only
// broke once the left factor was extracted into a named function whose return
// type is deduced -- the product then had nothing else asserting complex.
//
// WHAT THE VALUES ASSERT.
//   - `frequency: 1/day` times `time: day` cancels with exact reciprocal
//     magnitudes, so the exponent passes through UNSCALED -- `exp(i*0.25)`, not
//     `exp(i*0.25*86400)`. A magnitude slip shows in the first digit.
//   - real/imag are degree 1, so they PRESERVE the signature: `vsum` is
//     annotated `Float<volts>` and would not typecheck if the component came
//     back bare, and `vmag` exercises the same components through sqrt.
//   - `arg` is an angle and drops the signature, per the same table.
// The residue twins that must still REJECT are unit-errors/017 (exp of a
// genuinely DIMENSIONED complex, BL3006) and unit-errors/018 (floor, which has
// no complex overload at any signature).
// ============================================================================
Unit second
Unit day = 86400 * second
Unit time: day
Unit frequency: 1 / day
Unit volts

let i = complex(0.0, 1.0)
let w: Float<frequency> = 0.25
let tz: Float<time> = 1.0

// Scalar position, units cancel: the result stays COMPLEX.
let z = exp(i * w * tz)
let zr = real(z)
let zi = imag(z)
let za = arg(z)
// EXPECT: z = (0.968912421710645,0.247403959254523)
// EXPECT: zr = 0.968912421710645
// EXPECT: zi = 0.247403959254523
// arg drops the signature; the angle IS the exponent, unscaled.
// EXPECT: za = 0.25

// A complex that genuinely CARRIES a signature: real/imag hand back components
// that still carry it. The `Float<volts>` ascription is the load-bearing part.
let v: Float<volts> = 3.0
let zv = complex(3.0, 4.0) * v
let vr = real(zv)
let vi = imag(zv)
let vsum: Float<volts> = vr + vi
let vmag = sqrt(vr * vr + vi * vi)
// EXPECT: zv = (9,12)
// EXPECT: vr = 9
// EXPECT: vi = 12
// EXPECT: vsum = 21
// EXPECT: vmag = 15
